New opt-in option `--refinement-extends` (default off; `refinement-extends = true` in a project file): a class or trait declaration in a refining module may add parent traits to the declaration it refines, as in `trait T ... extends S { }` (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/88). The parents of the refined declaration are kept and the added ones follow them. Every check that an added parent calls for is made in the refining module, also at text it inherits: members of the refined type and of the types below it that become overrides are checked against the parent's members, classes below it must implement the parent's bodiless members, inherited constructors must leave the parent's fields assigned, and calls that the new overrides put on a cycle are checked for termination. A member of an added parent may not capture a name used in inherited text, and a refinement may not make a trait a reference type. A library and its client must agree on the option. With the option off nothing changes: an `extends` clause after `...` is a parse error, as before.
